From e0fbf96ee58903cbfb6a79be9c067325090d008a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arena Agent <agent@arena.ai>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 09:12:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] i686 asm: .code16 sized_op for the whole 0F operand-width
 class

The .code16 prefix inversion relies on encoders marking sized_op
whenever they select an operand width. A whole class of two-byte-opcode
instructions only set it on their 16-bit path, so their 32-bit forms
lost the 0x66 prefix in .code16 and silently became 16-bit operations
(half-register writes) in real-mode code:

  cmov{cc}l    0f 44 c1    -> GAS: 66 0f 44 c1
  incl/decl    40 / 49     -> GAS: 66 40 / 66 49
  imull (2/3-operand forms)
  movzbl/movzwl/movsbl/movswl (32-bit destination forms)
  bsfl/bsrl, bswapl, btl/btsl/btrl/btcl, shldl/shrdl,
  popcntl/tzcntl/lzcntl

Found by a GAS byte-diff probe (c16all.s, 30 instructions covering the
0F class); every listed form differed. After the fix both c16all.s and
c16wide.s are byte-exact against GAS in .code16 AND unchanged in
.code32 (cmp on raw .text).

This class matters right now: the kernel's real-mode setup code is
compiled -m16 at -Os, where cmov (if-conversion), incl/decl and the
movz/movs widenings appear constantly.

Validation: cargo test 687; regression 200; correctness 50;
intrinsics 3; linker 86; asm-diff exact; fuzz 300/300 (O2+Os).
